,3).\n\n- For line B: anchor (1,3) and (2,4). The line passes through (3,5) (outside). So cannot cover (3,3).\n\n- For line C: anchor (1,4) and (2,2). The line passes through (3,0) (outside). So cannot cover (3,3).\n\nThus none of the lines can cover (3,3). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nMaybe we can assign (2,2) to a different line to allow line C to have a different slope that passes through (3,3). Let's try to reassign.\n\nGoal: find a line anchored at (1,4) that also passes through (3,3). Compute slope: (3-4)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, which is sunny. So line through (1,4) and (3,3) slope -0.5. Does it also pass through any point in column 2? At x=2, y = 4 + (-0.5)*(2-1) = 4 - 0.5 = 3.5, not integer. So line anchored at (1,4) and (3,3) does not pass through any integer point in column 2. That's okay; we can assign column 2 points to other lines. So we can have line C: (1,4) and (3,3). Then column 3 point (3,3) is covered.\n\nNow we need to cover column 2 points (2,2),(2,3),(2,4). We have lines A (1,2) anchored, B (1,3), D (1,5). We need to assign each column 2 point to a distinct line among A,B,D (since each line can have at most one point per column). Let's see possibilities:\n\n- For line A anchored at (1,2), we can pair with (2,3) as before (slope 1). Good.\n\n- For line B anchored at (1,3), we can pair with (2,4) (slope 1). Good.\n\n- For line D anchored at (1,5), we need to pair with (2,2) maybe? Compute slope: (2-5)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. So line D: (1,5) and (2,2) slope -3. Does it also pass through any other points? At x=3, y = 5 + (-3)*(3-1) = 5 -6 = -1 (outside). So line D covers (1,5) and (2,2). Good.\n\nNow we have covered all points in column 2: (2,3) by A, (2,4) by B, (2,2) by D.\n\nNow column 3: we have (3,2) not covered yet. Which line can cover (3,2)? Let's see lines:\n\n- A: (1,2) and (2,3) line slope 1, passes through (3,4) (outside). So cannot cover (3,2).\n\n- B: (1,3) and (2,4) line slope 1, passes through (3,5) (outside). So cannot cover (3,2).\n\n- C: (1,4) and (3,3) line slope -0.5, passes through (2,3.5) (non-integer). So cannot cover (3,2).\n\n- D: (1,5) and (2,2) line slope -3, passes through (3,-1) (outside). So cannot cover (3,2).\n\nThus (3,2) is uncovered. We need to adjust again.\n\nMaybe we can assign (3,2) to line A or B by changing the column 2 assignment. Let's try to assign (2,3) to line D maybe, and (2,2) to line A, etc.\n\nWe need to find a set of 4 lines anchored at (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) that collectively cover all points in columns 2-4. Let's try to systematically search for a solution.\n\nWe have 4 anchor points in column 1: a1=(1,2), a2=(1,3), a3=(1,4), a4=(1,5). For each line Li, we can choose at most one point from each column 2,3,4 (maybe none). The line must be straight, so the points must be collinear.\n\nWe need to cover points:\n\n- Column 2: b1=(2,2), b2=(2,3), b3=(2,4)\n- Column 3: c1=(3,2), c2=(3,3)\n- Column 4: d1=(4,2)\n\nWe need to assign each of these points to some line Li such that for each line Li, the set of points assigned to it (including its anchor) are collinear.\n\nWe can treat each line as determined by its anchor and one other point (since any two points define a line). If we assign more than one extra point to a line, they must be collinear with the anchor and each other.\n\nThus each line can be defined by anchor + one other point (maybe from column 2,3,4). If we assign two extra points to a line, they must be collinear with anchor, which imposes constraints.\n\nGiven we have 4 lines and 7 points to assign (b1,b2,b3,c1,c2,d1), we need to assign at most one extra point per line? Actually we have 4 lines, each can take up to 3 extra points (one per column). But we have only 3 columns beyond column 1 (2,3,4). So each line can take at most 3 extra points (one per column). So we could assign each line to cover one point in each column, but we have only 4 lines and 3 columns, so we could assign each line to cover a point in each column, but we have 7 points, not 12. So some lines will have fewer points.\n\nGoal: find assignment such that each line's points are collinear.\n\nLet's try to find a line that can cover a point in column 2 and column 3 simultaneously (i.e., a line passing through anchor in column 1, a point in column 2, and a point in column 3). That would be efficient.\n\nConsider anchor a1=(1,2). Choose point b2=(2,3). The line through these has slope 1, equation y = x+1. At x=3, y=4, which is not a point in column 3 (c1=(3,2) or c2=(3,3)). So cannot cover column 3.\n\nAnchor a1 with b3=(2,4): slope = (4-2)/(2-1)=2, line y=2x? Actually line through (1,2) slope 2: y-2 = 2(x-1) => y = 2x. At x=3, y=6 (outside). So cannot cover column 3.\n\nAnchor a1 with b1=(2,2): slope = (2-2)/(2-1)=0, horizontal line y=2, which is non-sunny (slope 0). So not allowed.\n\nThus a1 cannot cover any column 3 point while also covering a column 2 point (except maybe b2 and c2? Let's check anchor a2=(1,3). Pair with b2=(2,3): slope = (3-3)/(2-1)=0, horizontal, not allowed. Pair with b3=(2,4): slope = (4-3)/(2-1)=1, line y = x+2? Actually y-3 = 1*(x-1) => y = x+2. At x=3, y=5 (outside). So cannot cover column 3.\n\nAnchor a2 with b1=(2,2): slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, slope -1, not allowed (non-sunny). So a2 cannot cover any column 3 point while also covering a column 2 point.\n\nAnchor a3=(1,4). Pair with b2=(2,3): slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. Pair with b3=(2,4): slope = (4-4)/(2-1)=0, not allowed. Pair with b1=(2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, line y = -2x + 6? Actually y-4 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x + 6. At x=3, y = 0 (outside). So cannot cover column 3.\n\nAnchor a4=(1,5). Pair with b2=(2,3): slope = (3-5)/(2-1) = -2, line y = -2x + 7? Actually y-5 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x + 7. At x=3, y = 1 (outside). So cannot cover column 3. Pair with b3=(2,4): slope = (4-5)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. Pair with b1=(2,2): slope = (2-5)/(2-1) = -3, line y = -3x + 8? At x=3, y = -1 (outside). So cannot cover column 3.\n\nThus any line that covers a point in column 2 and also an anchor in column 1 cannot also cover a point in column 3 (except maybe some special combination). Let's check anchor a1 with b2 and c1? Already we saw line through (1,2) and (2,3) has slope 1, at x=3 gives y=4, not c1 or c2. Anchor a1 with b3 and c2? (1,2) and (2,4) slope 2, at x=3 gives y=6, not c2. Anchor a1 with b1 and c1? (1,2) and (2,2) slope 0 (non-sunny). So no.\n\nThus each line can cover at most one point from columns 2-4 (maybe two if they are collinear with anchor but not both from different columns). Actually we could have a line that covers a point in column 3 and column 4 (skipping column 2). Let's check anchor a1 with c1=(3,2): slope = (2-2)/(3-1)=0, horizontal, not allowed. Anchor a1 with c2=(3,3): slope = (3-2)/(3-1)=1/2, line y-2 = 0.5(x-1) => y = 0.5x + 1.5. At x=2, y = 2.5 (not integer). So line a1-c2 does not pass through any column 2 point. It could also pass through d1? At x=4, y = 0.5*4 + 1.5 = 2 + 1.5 = 3.5 (not integer). So line a1-c2 covers only a1 and c2.\n\nAnchor a2 with c1: slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, line y-3 = -0.5(x-1) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5 (not integer). So no column 2 point.\n\nAnchor a2 with c2: slope = (3-3)/(3-1)=0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\nAnchor a3 with c1: slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nAnchor a3 with c2: slope = (3-4)/(3-1) = -1/2, line y-4 = -0.5(x-1) => y = -0.5x + 4.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 4.5 = 3.5 (not integer). So no column 2.\n\nAnchor a4 with c1: slope = (2-5)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, line y-5 = -1.5(x-1) => y = -1.5x + 6.5. At x=2, y = -3 + 6.5 = 3.5 (not integer). So no column 2.\n\nAnchor a4 with c2: slope = (3-5)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus any line anchored at column 1 cannot cover both a column 2 point and a column 3 point (except maybe some special combination we haven't considered). So each line can cover at most one point from columns 2-4. Since we have 4 lines, we can cover at most 4 points from columns 2-4. But we have 7 points to cover. So it's impossible to cover all points with 4 sunny lines plus one horizontal line covering y=1. So k=4 is impossible for n=5 with horizontal line as non-sunny. Could we use a different non-sunny line, like a vertical line at x=2? That would cover column 2 entirely (points (2,1)-(2,4)). Then we would have remaining points: column 1 (5 points), column 3 (3 points), column 4 (2 points), column 5 (1 point). That's 5+3+2+1 = 11 points to cover with 4 sunny lines. Column 1 has 5 points, but we have only 4 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point in column 1, so we cannot cover all 5 points in column 1. So vertical line at x=2 not enough.\n\nWhat about using a non-sunny line of slope -1 (i.e., line x+y=c). That can cover points across columns, but each such line can intersect each column at most one point (since x+y=c => y=c-x). So it's similar to a sunny line in terms of column coverage (one per column). However, slope -1 is non-sunny, but it can cover many points across columns. Could we use a slope -1 line to cover many points, reducing the need for sunny lines? Let's try to see if we can have k=4 with one non-sunny line of slope -1 that covers many points, and 4 sunny lines covering the rest.\n\nWe need to cover all points in T_5. Let's consider line L0: x+y=6 (the boundary line). This line includes points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). All these points are in T_5. So a single non-sunny line of slope -1 can cover 5 points (the \"diagonal\" from (1,5) to (5,1)). Then we have remaining points: column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) (4 points). Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) (3 points). Column 3: (3,1),(3,2) (2 points). Column 4: (4,1) (1 point). That's total 4+3+2+1 = 10 points left. We have 4 sunny lines to cover them. Column 1 has 4 points, so each sunny line must cover exactly one point in column 1. So each sunny line anchored at (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Then we need to cover points in columns 2-4 with these lines.\n\nNow we have 4 lines anchored at (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). We need to cover points:\n\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\n- Column 3: (3,1),(3,2)\n- Column 4: (4,1)\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So we can cover at most 4 points in column 2, but we have 3 points, okay. Column 3: at most 4 points, we have 2, okay. Column 4: at most 4 points, we have 1, okay. So it's possible in principle.\n\nNow we need to assign points to lines such that each line's points are collinear.\n\nLet's try to assign:\n\nLine A anchored at (1,1). It could also cover (2,2) (slope = (2-1)/(2-1)=1). Then line A passes through (3,3) (but (3,3) is already covered by the slope -1 line). So line A covers (1,1),(2,2). It could also cover (4,4) (outside). So line A cannot cover any other points in T_5 besides (2,2). So line A covers (2,2).\n\nLine B anchored at (1,2). Could cover (2,3) (slope = (3-2)/(2-1)=1). Then line B passes through (3,4) (outside). So line B covers (2,3).\n\nLine C anchored at (1,3). Could cover (2,1) (slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2). Then line C passes through (3,-1) (outside). So line C covers (2,1).\n\nLine D anchored at (1,4). Could cover (2,1) already used, maybe (2,2) used, (2,3) used. Could cover (3,1) maybe? Let's compute slope from (1,4) to (3,1): (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. So line D could cover (3,1). Then line D also passes through (4,-0.5) (outside). So line D covers (3,1). But we still need to cover (3,2) and (4,1). We have used all 4 lines; we can assign additional points to them if they are collinear.\n\nCheck line A: (1,1) and (2,2) slope 1. At x=3, y=3 (outside because (3,3) is covered by slope -1 line). At x=4, y=4 (outside). So line A cannot cover (3,2) or (4,1).\n\nLine B: (1,2) and (2,3) slope 1. At x=3, y=4 (outside). So cannot cover (3,2) or (4,1).\n\nLine C: (1,3) and (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. At x=3, y = -2*(3-1)+3 = -4+3 = -1 (outside). So cannot cover (3,2) or (4,1).\n\nLine D: (1,4) and (3,1) slope -1.5. At x=2, y = -1.5*(2-1)+4 = 2.5 (not integer). At x=4, y = -1.5*(4-1)+4 = -4.5+4 = -0.5 (outside). So cannot cover (4,1) or (3,2).\n\nThus we cannot cover (3,2) and (4,1). Could we reassign differently? Let's try different assignments.\n\nGoal: cover (3,2) and (4,1). Perhaps we can assign (3,2) to line anchored at (1,2) with slope (2-2)/(3-1)=0 (horizontal) not allowed. Or anchored at (1,3) with slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, line y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5 (not integer). So cannot cover (2,?) integer. So line anchored at (1,3) cannot cover any column 2 point while also covering (3,2). So maybe we need a line anchored at (1,4) to cover (3,2). Slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nThus (3,2) cannot be covered by any line anchored at column 1 with a point in column 2 (since slope would be -1 or 0). But maybe we can cover (3,2) by a line anchored at (1,1) and (2,? )? Let's see line anchored at (1,1) and (2,? ) to also pass through (3,2). Solve for b such that line through (1,1) and (2,b) passes through (3,2). The slope is (b-1)/(2-1) = b-1. Equation: y-1 = (b-1)*(x-1). At x=3, y = 1 + (b-1)*(2) = 1 + 2(b-1) = 2b -1. Set equal to 2 => 2b -1 = 2 => 2b = 3 => b = 1.5, not integer. So cannot.\n\nLine anchored at (1,2) and (2,b) to pass through (3,2): slope = (b-2)/(2-1) = b-2. Equation: y-2 = (b-2)*(x-1). At x=3, y = 2 + (b-2)*2 = 2 + 2b -4 = 2b -2. Set =2 => 2b -2 = 2 => 2b = 4 => b = 2. So line through (1,2) and (2,2) (horizontal) slope 0, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nLine anchored at (1,3) and (2,b) to pass through (3,2): slope = (b-3). Equation: y-3 = (b-3)*(x-1). At x=3, y = 3 + (b-3)*2 = 3 + 2b -6 = 2b -3. Set =2 => 2b -3 = 2 => 2b = 5 => b = 2.5, not integer.\n\nLine anchored at (1,4) and (2,b) to pass through (3,2): slope = (b-4). At x=3, y = 4 + (b-4)*2 = 4 + 2b -8 = 2b -4. Set =2 => 2b -4 = 2 => 2b = 6 => b = 3. So line through (1,4) and (2,3) slope = (3-4) = -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nThus (3,2) cannot be covered by any line anchored at column 1 with a column 2 point, unless the line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny. So (3,2) must be covered by a line that does not have an anchor in column 1. But we have only 4 sunny lines, each anchored at column 1 because we need to cover column 1's 4 points. However, we could have a sunny line that does not pass through column 1 but covers some column 1 points? Actually each column 1 point must be covered by some line. If we have a sunny line that does not pass through column 1, then some column 1 point must be covered by a non-sunny line. But we have only one non-sunny line (the slope -1 line). That line already covers (1,5) which is not in the remaining set (since we removed diagonal). Actually after using the slope -1 line x+y=6, we have column 1 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). The slope -1 line does not cover any of these (since x+y=6 => (1,5) not in remaining). So we need to cover these 4 points with sunny lines. So each sunny line must pass through one of these points. So each sunny line is anchored at column 1. So we cannot cover (3,2) as we saw. So k=4 impossible for n=5.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k≥4 impossible seems plausible. However, we need to verify for all n≥3. The solution's argument for k≥4 is not rigorous; they claim \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This reasoning seems flawed because it assumes that we need vertical lines for columns not covered by sunny lines, but we could use horizontal or slope -1 lines to cover multiple columns. However, the non-sunny lines are limited to n-k lines, which could be used as vertical/horizontal/slope -1 lines. The argument that we need at least n-4 vertical lines is not justified. So there may be a flaw in the reasoning for k≥4.\n\nBut we need to check if the answer {0,1,3} is indeed correct. Let's try to see if there is any possible configuration with k=4 for some n≥3. Let's try n=6. Maybe we can find a configuration with k=4. Let's attempt to construct a configuration for n=6 with k=4. If we can find a counterexample, the answer is wrong. If we cannot find, maybe answer is correct.\n\nWe need to find n distinct lines covering all points (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1 = 7. So T_6 points: columns:\n\n- x=1: y=1..6 (6 points)\n- x=2: y=1..5 (5 points)\n- x=3: y=1..4 (4 points)\n- x=4: y=1..3 (3 points)\n- x=5: y=1..2 (2 points)\n- x=6: y=1 (1 point)\n\nTotal points = 6+5+4+3+2+1 = 21 = n(n+1)/2 = 6*7/2 = 21.\n\nWe need 6 lines total. Suppose we try k=4 sunny lines, 2 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines could be vertical/horizontal/slope -1. We need to cover all points.\n\nWe can try to use a slope -1 line x+y=7 (the boundary) covering points (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). That's 6 points. Then we have remaining points: column 1: (1,1)-(1,5) (5 points), column 2: (2,1)-(2,4) (4 points), column 3: (3,1)-(3,3) (3 points), column 4: (4,1)-(4,2) (2 points), column 5: (5,1) (1 point). That's total 5+4+3+2+1 = 15 points left. We have 4 sunny lines left (since total lines =6, we used 1 non-sunny line). So we need to cover 15 points with 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across 5 columns (1-5) each line can cover at most 5 points. So 4 lines can cover at most 20 points, which is enough for 15 points. However, we also need to cover column 1's 5 points, which would require each sunny line to cover exactly one point in column 1. So each sunny line must be anchored at a distinct point in column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) maybe (1,5) also? Actually we have 5 points in column 1, but only 4 sunny lines, so one of those points must be covered by a non-sunny line. But we already used the non-sunny line x+y=7, which does not cover any of those column 1 points (since (1,6) is covered, not (1,1)-(1,5)). So we need another non-sunny line (vertical/horizontal/slope -1) to cover the remaining column 1 point(s). But we only have 2 non-sunny lines total (since k=4). We used one (the diagonal). So we have one more non-sunny line left. That line could be vertical at x=1, covering all column 1 points. That would cover (1,1)-(1,6). But (1,6) is already covered by diagonal, but that's okay. So we could use vertical line x=1 as the second non-sunny line. Then we have 4 sunny lines left to cover the remaining points: columns 2-5 (since column 1 is covered by vertical line). Let's see if we can cover all points in columns 2-5 with 4 sunny lines.\n\nRemaining points after covering column 1 (by vertical line) and diagonal (x+y=7) are:\n\n- Column 2: (2,1)-(2,4) (4 points)\n- Column 3: (3,1)-(3,3) (3 points)\n- Column 4: (4,1)-(4,2) (2 points)\n- Column 5: (5,1) (1 point)\n\nTotal = 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. We have 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across columns 2-5 (4 columns), each line can cover at most 4 points. So 4 lines can cover at most 16 points, enough for 10 points. However, we need to ensure that each column's points can be assigned to distinct lines such that each line's points are collinear.\n\nWe have 4 columns (2,3,4,5) and 4 lines. Column 2 has 4 points, so each line must cover exactly one point in column 2. Column 5 has 1 point, so one line will cover that point. Column 4 has 2 points, so two lines will cover those. Column 3 has 3 points, so three lines will cover those. So we need to assign points to lines such that each line gets at most one point per column, and the points assigned to each line (including possibly multiple columns) are collinear.\n\nWe can try to find such assignment. Let's denote the 4 sunny lines as L1, L2, L3, L4. They need to each pass through a point in column 2 (distinct). Let's assign:\n\n- L1: (2,1)\n- L2: (2,2)\n- L3: (2,3)\n- L4: (2,4)\n\nNow we need to assign points in column 3 (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) to these lines, each line can take at most one point in column 3. So we need to assign each of these three points to three of the lines. Similarly column 4 points (4,1),(4,2) to two lines, column 5 point (5,1) to one line.\n\nWe need to ensure collinearity: For each line Li, the points assigned to it (including its anchor in column 2) must be collinear.\n\nThus we need to find lines passing through (2,i) and some point in column 3, maybe also column 4, etc.\n\nLet's try to find a line through (2,1) and (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1. So line L1: passes through (2,1),(3,2). At x=4, y = 3 (since slope 1, intercept? Equation: y-1 = 1*(x-2) => y = x-1. At x=4, y=3 => (4,3) is not in T_6 (since column 4 has only y=1,2). So L1 cannot cover (4,1) or (4,2). At x=5, y=4 (outside). So L1 covers (2,1),(3,2). Good.\n\nLine L2: anchor (2,2). Could pair with (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed (slope -1 is non-sunny). Could pair with (3,3): slope = (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, line y-2 = 1*(x-2) => y = x. At x=4, y=4 (outside). So L2 can cover (3,3). So L2: (2,2),(3,3). Good.\n\nLine L3: anchor (2,3). Could pair with (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, line y-3 = -2*(x-2) => y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1 (outside). So L3 covers (3,1). Could also pair with (3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. Could pair with (3,3): slope = 0, not allowed. So L3 can cover (3,1). Good.\n\nLine L4: anchor (2,4). Could pair with (3,2): slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, line y-4 = -2*(x-2) => y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0 (outside). Could pair with (3,3): slope = (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. Could pair with (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, line y-4 = -3*(x-2) => y = -3x + 10. At x=4, y = -12 + 10 = -2 (outside). So L4 can pair with (3,2) maybe? Actually slope -2 is okay. So L4: (2,4),(3,2). Good.\n\nNow we have assigned column 3 points: (3,2) assigned to L4, (3,3) assigned to L2, (3,1) assigned to L3. So all column 3 points covered.\n\nNow column 4 points: (4,1),(4,2). We need to assign them to lines that can accommodate them while preserving collinearity.\n\nCheck each line:\n\n- L1: currently (2,1),(3,2). Equation y = x-1. At x=4, y=3 (not (4,1) or (4,2)). So L1 cannot cover any column 4 point.\n\n- L2: currently (2,2),(3,3). Equation y = x. At x=4, y=4 (outside). So L2 cannot cover column 4 points.\n\n- L3: currently (2,3),(3,1). Equation: slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, intercept? y-3 = -2*(x-2) => y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1 (outside). So L3 cannot cover column 4 points.\n\n- L4: currently (2,4),(3,2). Equation: slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, y-4 = -2*(x-2) => y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0 (outside). So L4 cannot cover column 4 points.\n\nThus none of the lines can cover column 4 points. We could try to adjust assignments to allow some line to pass through a column 4 point.\n\nPerhaps we can assign column 4 points to lines anchored at column 2 points but with different slopes. Let's try to reassign.\n\nGoal: find lines through (2,i) and (4,j) for some i,j such that slope is not 0,∞,-1. Then we can also try to include a column 3 point on the same line if possible.\n\nLet's consider line through (2,1) and (4,1): slope = (1-1)/(4-2) = 0, not allowed. (2,1) and (4,2): slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2, sunny. Equation: y-1 = 0.5*(x-2) => y = 0.5x. At x=3, y = 1.5 (not integer). So line (2,1)-(4,2) does not pass through any column 3 integer point. So we could assign (2,1) to (4,2) and maybe also (3,?)? No integer point. So L1 could be (2,1),(4,2). Then column 3 point (3,?) not covered by L1. That's okay; we can assign column 3 points to other lines.\n\nNow we need to cover column 3 points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). Let's try to assign them to lines anchored at (2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Let's see possibilities:\n\n- L2 anchored at (2,2). Could pair with (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. Pair with (3,2): slope = 0, not allowed. Pair with (3,3): slope = 1, sunny. So L2: (2,2),(3,3). At x=4,